#!/usr/bin/env -S python3 -I
"""clx - auto-switching Claude Code launcher across several subscription accounts.

Each account is one Claude Code config dir:
    ~/.claude            the default dir (config file is ~/.claude.json, NOT inside it)
    ~/.claude-<name>     one per extra account (config file is <dir>/.claude.json)

`clx` picks the account with head-room left on the 5-hour limit and execs the real
`claude` with CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR pointed at a per-session dir ~/.clx-sessions/<pid>
whose .credentials.json is a SYMLINK into the chosen account's dir.

Why a session dir: Claude Code re-reads <CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR>/.credentials.json on
EVERY request and never falls back to a cached token (proven 2026-09-03 against a
long-lived process), so re-pointing that one symlink moves a RUNNING session to
another account - no restart, no --continue, subagents and background tasks keep
going. `clx --handoff-check` (the hook) does exactly that when the account this
session runs on has spent its 5-hour limit.

Why not a token: CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN / apiKeyHelper were tested and are static
or hang. CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR keeps Claude Code's own login and token refresh.

Files it owns (all 0600):
    ~/.claude/cc.json               {"threshold": 90, "primary": "<name>", "paused": {...}}
    ~/.claude/cc-usage-cache.json   per-account /usage snapshot, 180 s TTL
    ~/.claude/active-account.json   what the last launch chose, read by the statusline
    ~/.claude/clx-handoff.log       one line per in-process hand-off and heal
    ~/.clx-sessions/<pid>/          one live REPL's CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR (0700)

No third-party dependencies: stdlib only.
"""

